# Precise file edits

Prefer `edit_apply` over rewriting a whole file for any change to a file that
already exists. Whole-file rewrites land on stale content and quietly reformat
code you never meant to touch.

## The loop

1. Find the range with `edit_locate`. Do not count lines by eye.
2. Call `file_read` for the lines you need, and keep the `hash` it returns.
3. Call `edit_apply` with that hash as `base_hash` and your edits.

Line numbers are 1-based and inclusive. All edits in one call refer to the same
snapshot, so they must not overlap.

```json
{
  "path": "src/app.py",
  "base_hash": "sha256:9f2c…",
  "edits": [
    { "op": "replace", "range": { "start_line": 42, "end_line": 47 },
      "new_text": "def fetch_user(self, user_id: str) -> User:\n    …\n" },
    { "op": "insert", "after_line": 10, "new_text": "from pydantic import BaseModel\n" }
  ]
}
```

Operations: `replace` a range, `insert` after a line (`after_line: 0` is the top
of the file), `delete` a range.

## Finding the range with edit_locate

Use `by: "symbol"` in Python and `by: "heading"` in Markdown. Both return
`start_line`, `end_line`, and the file `hash`, so one call is enough.

```json
{ "path": "src/users/service.py", "by": "symbol", "name": "UserService.fetch_user" }
{ "path": "README.md", "by": "heading", "text": "Installation" }
```

- `by: "symbol"` takes a dotted name and uses the parser's own line info. A
  bare `fetch_user` works when only one function has that name.
- `by: "heading"` returns the section through the next heading of the same or
  higher level, so subsections come with it. Pass `include_subsections: false`
  to stop at the first subheading. A `#` inside a fenced code block is not a
  heading and will not be found.
- `by: "anchor"` (the default) finds `text` verbatim or a `regex`. If the text
  appears more than once the call fails as ambiguous and lists where it matched;
  pass `occurrence: 0` to take the first, `1` for the second, and so on.

**A symbol range includes decorators.** Replacing a decorated function with
`new_text` that omits the decorator will delete it. Include it in your
replacement.

## Indentation

`edit_apply` defaults to `indent_mode: "relative"`: the common margin is removed
from `new_text` and the indentation of the surrounding block is re-applied. Write
the code flush-left and it lands at the right depth.

Your `new_text` must still be correct *relative to itself*. This lands correctly:

```text
def fetch_user(self, uid):
    return {"id": uid}
```

This does not, because the body has no step of its own:

```text
def fetch_user(self, uid):
return {"id": uid}
```

Pass `indent_mode: "absolute"` to write `new_text` exactly as sent.

## Checking your work before you write

`edit_apply` returns a `diagnostics` block on every call:

```json
"diagnostics": {
  "new": [{ "tool": "ruff", "code": "F841", "message": "...", "line": 4 }],
  "new_count": 1,
  "new_error_count": 1,
  "preexisting_count": 12,
  "resolved_count": 0
}
```

`new` holds only the problems **your edit introduced**. Problems that were already
in the file are counted in `preexisting_count` and never reported as yours, so a
file with existing lint debt stays editable. A problem that merely moved down a
line because of your insert is not new either.

`stages` says what actually ran. A stage that could not run appears as
`"skipped"` with the reason, so a clean result is never mistaken for an
unvalidated one.

Use `validate_file` to check a file, or a block of proposed text, without
touching anything:

```json
{ "path": "src/app.py" }
{ "path": "src/app.py", "content": "def check():\n    return 1\n" }
```

For deeper checks, use `type_check` to run `pyright` or `mypy` on a
file or a block of proposed content. Type checking is opt-in because
it is slower and noisier on a project that has not configured for it.

```json
{ "path": "src/app.py" }
{ "path": "src/app.py", "tool": "mypy" }
{ "path": "src/app.py", "content": "def f(x: int) -> int:\n    return x" }
```

`tool` is `auto` (default; tries Pyright, then mypy), `pyright`, or
`mypy`. A missing tool returns `skipped: true` with the reason, never
zero findings — silence here is the worst possible output, because a
clean answer from a tool that was never asked is indistinguishable from
a clean file.

For project-level tooling, use `config_detect` to ask "what governs
this file?" (or "what governs the workspace?" when called with no
`path`). The answer is the same shape as the `tooling` field on
`validate_file`; reading it before editing lets you plan a change
without committing to it.

When the project's tooling looks wrong, use `config_doctor` to
diagnose it. The doctor returns a list of findings, each with a
`severity`, `evidence`, an `explanation`, and a `suggested_patch` as a
unified diff. The doctor never writes. To apply a patch, pass its
`finding_id` and `confirmation_token` to `config_apply_fix`; the
token is the SHA-256 of the finding id and the workspace root, and
forces a re-run when the token is stale.

```json
{ "path": "src/app.py" }
{ }
{ "path": "pyproject.toml" }
{ "finding_id": "ruff_overlap::ruff::pyproject.toml::3",
  "confirmation_token": "<token from config_doctor response>" }
```

## Controls on `edit_apply`

| Option | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `validate` | `warn` | `off` skip the checks; `warn` write and report; `strict` refuse a new error |
| `format` | `range` | `none` leave spacing alone; `range` format only the edited lines; `full` format the whole file |
| `autofix` | `none` | `safe` apply Ruff's safe fixes on the edited lines and the import block |
| `require_all_stages` | `false` | In `strict`, also fail when a stage was skipped |

`validate: "off"` turns off the *checks* only — syntax and lint. `format` and
`autofix` are separate knobs and still run, so `validate: "off", format:
"range"` formats your edit without linting it.

Formatting is **range-contained**. Ruff's `--range` is used when available, so a
long function elsewhere in the file is never reformatted by your edit to line 10.

With `autofix: "safe"`, only Ruff's *safe* fixes are applied, and only to lines
your edit touched (plus the import block, which is where import sorting lives).
Unsafe fixes are never applied automatically.

## Guarding a risky edit

Add `expect` to any edit that depends on the current content of its range. It
must appear in that range, or the edit is rejected and you are shown what is
actually there:

```json
{ "op": "replace",
  "range": { "start_line": 42, "end_line": 47 },
  "expect": "def fetch_user(",
  "new_text": "…" }
```

Use it whenever the edit could do damage if the file moved.

## Before you commit

Pass `dry_run: true` to see the diff without writing.

## When something is rejected

Every rejection comes back with a code and a hint. Read the hint and fix the call
rather than retrying it unchanged.

| Code | What it means | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `STALE_HASH` | The file changed since your read | Re-read, then re-issue the edit |
| `ANCHOR_NOT_FOUND` | Nothing matched, or it matched ambiguously | The error lists candidates; use one, or pass `occurrence` |
| `RANGE_OUT_OF_BOUNDS` | Line numbers fall outside the file | Use the `line_count` in the error |
| `EXPECTED_TEXT_MISMATCH` | The range no longer contains your `expect` text | Use `current_text` from the error, or re-read |
| `OVERLAPPING_EDITS` | Two edits touch the same lines | Combine them into one edit, or split the batch |
| `SYNTAX_ERROR_INTRODUCED` | `strict` mode: the edit would not parse | Fix `new_text` and retry; the error has the line and column |
| `LINT_REGRESSION` | `strict` mode: the edit adds a new problem | Fix the named rules and retry |
| `PATH_FORBIDDEN` | Outside the workspace, or a protected file | Edit a different file, or ask the user |
| `FILE_EXISTS` | `file_create` found the path taken | Edit it with `edit_apply`, or pass `overwrite` |
| `NOTEBOOK_INVALID` | Not valid JSON, or fails the nbformat schema | Check the file is a real `.ipynb` |
| `SCRIPT_INVALID` | A `.py` file has no `# %%` cell markers | Use `edit_apply` on a regular `.py`, or add at least one `# %%` |
| `CELL_NOT_FOUND` | No cell with that id | The error lists the ids that exist; re-read `nb_outline` |
| `KERNEL_START_FAILED` | The kernel could not be started or connected | The error names the reason; check `kernel_discover` for installed kernelspecs |

`STALE_HASH` is a safety net working as intended, not an obstacle. It usually
means the user saved the file while you were working. Re-read and continue.

On `SYNTAX_ERROR_INTRODUCED` and `LINT_REGRESSION`, **fix the reported problem
and retry. Do not switch to `validate: "off"`** to get the edit through; that
leaves the user with a file you know is broken. If you genuinely need to write it,
say so and let the user decide.

## Notebooks

Never edit a `.ipynb` by targeting line numbers in its JSON. Use the notebook
tools.

Start with `nb_outline`, then use the `cell_id` it returns:

```json
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "cell_id": "a1b2c3d4", "include_outputs": true }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "cell_id": "a1b2c3d4", "source": "df = pd.read_csv(p)" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "cell_id": "a1b2c3d4",
  "range": { "start_line": 12, "end_line": 14 }, "source": "…" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "source": "## Cleaning", "cell_type": "markdown",
  "after_cell_id": "a1b2c3d4" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "cell_id": "e5f6a7b8", "destination": 0 }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "cell_id": "e5f6a7b8" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "cell_id": "a1b2c3d4" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "stop_on_error": true, "restart_kernel": false }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "cell_start": 3, "cell_end": 5 }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "action": "status" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "action": "restart" }
{ "path": "analysis.ipynb", "action": "shutdown" }
```

**Use `cell_id`, never `cell_number`, for edits.** Numbers shift the moment a
cell is inserted, deleted, or moved; ids do not. If you do use a number, re-read
`nb_outline` after any structural change.

**`range` is cell-relative.** Line 1 is the first line of that cell, not of the
file. Read the cell first with `nb_cell_read`, or you will guess wrong.

Changing a code cell's source clears its outputs and `execution_count`. That is
deliberate: a printed result that no longer matches the code is misleading. Pass
`keep_outputs: true` to keep them.

Notebook diagnostics come back as `cell_id` plus a cell-relative line, and
`validate: "strict"` refuses a change that introduces a new one. On
`LINT_REGRESSION`, fix the named cell and retry.

Cells are run on a real Python kernel, one per notebook. State from one cell is
visible to the next, the same way Jupyter behaves. The first execution starts
the kernel; subsequent calls reuse it. A kernel persists across the four
mutators and the four execution tools until `kernel_control: shutdown` or
until the server restarts.

`persist: true` (default) writes the cell's outputs back to the notebook. Pass
`persist: false` to execute without saving — useful for dry-running code.

`execute_all_cells` with `stop_on_error: true` halts at the first error;
`restart_kernel: true` discards the interpreter's state and starts fresh. A
markdown or raw cell inside an execution range is reported as `skipped`, not
run; passing one to `execute_cell` on its own returns `CELL_NOT_CODE`.

The per-cell `timeout_s` interrupts the kernel on expiry. The result's
`error.reason` is `"timeout"`; the kernel is otherwise unaffected. To break a
running cell by hand, call `kernel_control: interrupt`.

The execute tools also accept `connection_file`: the path to a Jupyter
connection file (the JSON document Jupyter writes when it starts a kernel).
This attaches the registry to a kernel that was started elsewhere, such as
one running on a JupyterHub server. Remote sessions are reported with
`kernel.remote: true`, and `restart` / `shutdown` on a remote session only
close the local channels — the kernel itself is the user's to end on its
host.

## Percent-format scripts

The same six cell tools and the same execute tools accept percent-format
`.py` files: a plain Python file whose lines starting with `# %%` mark cell
boundaries, the way VS Code and Spyder do.

```python
# %%
import os

# %% [markdown]
# Notes

# %% my cell
x = 1
```

A `.py` file with no `# %%` markers returns `SCRIPT_INVALID`; a regular
Python module is not silently turned into a virtual notebook. Cell types
are inferred from the marker: `# %%` is code, `# %% [markdown]` is
markdown, `# %% [raw]` is raw.

Cell ids are derived from the SHA-1 of the cell's source, so two reads of
the same file see the same id without an intermediate write. Editing a
cell's source changes its id; re-read the outline after an edit.

`persist` is forced to `false` for percent scripts because the file has
no on-disk location for outputs. Kernel state still persists across
calls.

## Kernel discovery

Before opening a notebook that names an unfamiliar kernelspec, call
`kernel_discover` to list the kernelspecs installed on this machine. The
result includes the language, the argv, and whether the interpreter is
reachable on `PATH`. The tool is read-only and never starts a kernel.

## Scope

This build has twenty tools: `file_read`, `edit_locate`, `edit_apply`,
`file_create`, `validate_file`, `type_check`, `config_detect`, `config_doctor`,
`config_apply_fix`, `nb_outline`, `nb_cell_read`, `nb_cell_insert`,
`nb_cell_update`, `nb_cell_delete`, `nb_cell_move`, `execute_cell`,
`execute_all_cells`, `execute_cells_in_range`, `kernel_control`,
and `kernel_discover`.

Validation covers Python and Markdown, plus notebook code cells. Other
file types are edited without a syntax or lint stage; `stages` will show
them as `skipped`. Percent-format `# %%` scripts are segmented as
notebook cells, with the same cell tools, execute tools, and validation
pipeline as `.ipynb` files. `type_check` is opt-in: it runs `pyright` or
`mypy` on demand and reports the same diagnostic shape as
`validate_file`.